375, -0.01000213623046875, 0.00972747802734375, 0.027130126953125, 0.0369873046875, 0.045928955078125, 0.0147247314453125, 0.0033626556396484375, 0.021240234375, 0.007373809814453125, -0.03656005859375, 0.0027866363525390625, -2.1457672119140625e-06, 0.0183868408203125, -0.016937255859375, -0.01605224609375, 0.00762939453125, -0.0182647705078125, -0.0230712890625, 0.015533447265625, -0.01346588134765625, 0.0038394927978515625, -0.001758575439453125, -0.0169525146484375, -0.0174102783203125, 0.008453369140625, -0.006008148193359375, -0.038970947265625, 0.01546478271484375, -0.003429412841796875, 0.0293731689453125, 0.019775390625, -0.05224609375, -0.0241241455078125, -0.005619049072265625, 0.007732391357421875, -0.004878997802734375, 0.0007205009460449219, 0.0179901123046875, -0.0047454833984375, 0.01279449462890625, -0.0276031494140625, -0.016082763671875, 0.0308990478515625, -0.0188446044921875, 0.011932373046875, 0.0175018310546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, with particular attention to electrical circuits. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations governing a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process that is well-established in classical analysis but not yet fully developed within the framework of GA. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras\u2014such as quaternions [1] and Clifford algebras [2,3]\u2014a comprehensive definition and analysis within the broader context of geometric algebras remains lacking. Specifically, prior studies have either focused on special cases or have not addressed the full range of multivector-valued functions encountered in GA-based modeling. For example, quaternionic Laplace transforms [1] are limited to four-dimensional division algebras and do not generalize to arbitrary multivector structures. In the Clifford algebra context, Helmstetter and Micali [2] and Hestenes and Sobczyk [3] discuss Laplace and Fourier transforms primarily for scalar or vector-valued functions, often imposing algebraic constraints such as requiring the function to commute with the Laplace variable or restricting attention to subalgebras where the exponential is well-defined. These constraints limit the applicability of their approaches to general multivector-valued functions, particularly when higher-grade elements are present or when the algebra is not division. For instance, Helmstetter and Micali [2] provide a Laplace transform for Clifford algebra-valued functions but require the Laplace variable to be a scalar and focus on cases where the function components commute with the Laplace variable, while Hestenes and Sobczyk [3] primarily address Fourier transforms and restrict attention to scalar and vector parts, leaving the treatment of general multivector-valued functions incomplete.\n\nThe specific research question addressed in this work is: How can the Laplace transform be rigorously defined and systematically applied to multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, and what properties does this transform preserve relative to the classical case? Our novel contribution is the introduction of a general definition of the Laplace transform for geometric algebras of arbitrary signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5, together with a detailed analysis of its properties and application to practical systems. Unlike previous approaches, our definition applies directly to all multivector-valued functions without requiring commutativity between the Laplace variable and the function or restricting to subalgebras. By treating the Laplace variable as a scalar and applying the transform componentwise to each scalar coefficient in the multivector expansion, we overcome the limitations of prior work and provide a unified framework that accommodates both scalar and multivector components, enabling the direct use of GA in the analysis of dynamical systems.\n\nWe restrict our attention to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated by both computational and theoretical considerations. Computationally, algebras with p + q \u2264 5 have dimension at most 32, allowing explicit representation and manipulation of multivectors with standard computational resources; the number of basis blades grows exponentially with p + q, making higher-dimensional algebras infeasible for symbolic or numerical computation. Theoretically, higher-dimensional algebras can exhibit algebraic pathologies relevant to the Laplace transform context, such as the emergence of non-simple ideals, a nontrivial center, and the presence of zero divisors, which can complicate the definition and properties of integral transforms and interfere with the existence and uniqueness of Laplace transforms for multivector-valued functions. Moreover, most applications in engineering and physics do not require higher-dimensional algebras, so this restriction does not significantly limit the generality of our results.\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 1 reviews related work on Laplace transforms in hypercomplex and Clifford algebras, highlighting the limitations of existing approaches and motivating the need for a general GA-based framework. Section 2 introduces our formal definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, together with the main existence and linearity theorems. Section 3 demonstrates the preservation of key properties, such as linearity, the treatment of derivatives (i.e., the Laplace transform of the derivative corresponds to multiplication by the Laplace variable, with initial conditions encoded as multivectors), and the handling of initial conditions. Section 4 presents an illustrative example: the analysis of an RLC circuit modeled within GA, emphasizing the unification of scalar and multivector components and the simplification of the solution process. We conclude in Section 5 with a discussion of future research directions, potential applications, and limitations of the current approach.\n\n**1. Related Work**\n\nQuaternionic Laplace transforms [1] are defined for quaternion-valued functions, but their applicability is limited to four-dimensional division algebras and does not extend to the full range of multivector structures found in geometric algebras. Helmstetter and Micali [2] develop Laplace and Fourier transforms for Clifford algebra-valued functions, but their approach requires the Laplace variable to be a scalar and often restricts attention to cases where the function components commute with the Laplace variable. This limits the generality of their results, particularly for functions with higher-grade components. Hestenes and Sobczyk [3] focus primarily on Fourier transforms in Clifford algebras, providing operational rules for scalar and vector-valued functions but not addressing the full generality of multivector-valued functions. In both [2] and [3], the treatment of the Laplace transform is either limited in scope or imposes algebraic constraints that restrict its applicability to general GA-based modeling.\n\n**2. Definition of the Laplace Transform in Geometric Algebra**\n\nOur formal definition of the Laplace transform in GA is as follows: For a multivector-valued function \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q), the Laplace transform is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter. Here, the exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is always a scalar function and acts by scalar multiplication on each scalar coefficient in the multivector expansion of \\( f(t) \\); that is, if \\( f(t) = \\sum_A f_A(t) e_A \\) with \\( e_A \\) basis blades and \\( f_A(t) \\) real- or complex-valued functions, then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\sum_A \\left( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f_A(t) dt \\right) e_A.\n\\]\nThus, the Laplace transform is computed by applying the classical scalar Laplace transform to each scalar coefficient \\( f_A(t) \\), and the blades \\( e_A \\) remain unchanged. This approach requires that the Laplace variable \\( s \\) be a real or complex scalar, not a general multivector, to ensure the exponential is well-defined and the integral converges as in the classical case. Unlike previous Clifford algebra treatments, our definition does not require the function to commute with the Laplace variable or restrict attention to subalgebras, and it applies directly to all multivector-valued functions.\n\n**3. Properties and Proof Techniques**\n\nWe prove that this transform is linear, i.e., \\( \\mathcal{L}[af + bg] = a\\mathcal{L}[f] + b\\mathcal{L}[g] \\) for scalars \\( a, b \\), and that it exists for functions \\( f \\) whose scalar components \\( f_A(t) \\) are piecewise continuous and of exponential order, analogous to the classical case. Specifically, if there exist constants \\( M, \\alpha \\) such that \\( |f_A(t)| \\leq M e^{\\alpha t} \\) for all \\( t \\geq 0 \\) and all \\( A \\), then \\( \\mathcal{L}[f](s) \\) exists for \\( \\operatorname{Re}(s) > \\alpha \\). Furthermore, we establish that the Laplace transform of the derivative satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f'](s) = s\\mathcal{L}[f](s) - f(0),\n\\]\nwhere \\( f(0) \\) is a multivector initial condition, thus preserving the classical operational property. The proofs of these properties proceed by applying the classical results to each scalar component \\( f_A(t) \\), leveraging the linearity of integration and the direct sum structure of the geometric algebra. Detailed proofs and technical lemmas supporting these results are provided in Appendix A, where we outline the componentwise application of classical Laplace transform theorems and address the preservation of operational calculus for all multivector grades.\n\nA key technical challenge in extending the Laplace transform to GA is ensuring that the operational calculus (e.g., differentiation, initial value encoding) remains valid for all multivector grades, which is achieved here by restricting the Laplace variable to scalars and by working componentwise. This avoids complications that would arise if the Laplace variable were allowed to be a general multivector, where the exponential function may not be well-defined or may not commute with all elements of the algebra.\n\n**4. Illustrative Example: RLC Circuit in Geometric Algebra**\n\nTo demonstrate the practical utility of our framework, we analyze a series RLC circuit where voltages and currents are represented"}}